      "note": "1 minute. Three states, one bullet per click; the takeaway arrives with the last. Why the paper exists. Spanner started as a key-value store: in the paper's words NoSQL methods for point lookups and range scans, plus multi-row transactions and reads at a timestamp. By the 2012 paper it already had schematized tables, interleaving, which stores a child table's rows inside its parent's, and a SQL-based query language, so SQL is not new here; what is new is how deep it goes. Google developers on Bigtable lacked a schema, cross-row transactions, consistent replication and a real query language. Megastore put transactions on top of Bigtable but still had no robust query language, so application code did its own joins and aggregations. The workload is both kinds at once: OLTP, point lookups and small joins in milliseconds, and analytics, long scans and aggregations taking seconds to hours. Say aloud, since the slide no longer shows it: the first query engine was bolted on, using high-level APIs like an external application. That is the paper's own confession about its first version, and it sets up the choice on slide 4: SQL bolted on beside the store, or built into it. First, the next slide lines this lecture up against last time's. \n Source: §1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
