    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Five states: the System R column alone, then Spanner's side one row per click. The bridge from last time, one row at a time. Data: System R's tables sat on one machine's disk; Spanner's are cut into key ranges, shards, each replicated across datacenters. Cost: last time we counted page fetches; today the expensive thing is a call to another machine and the rows it ships back. Last lecture's recap promised that the cost measure would grow a network term and a shard fan-out term; slide 14 says honestly what the paper does with that, which is less than you might expect. When the plan is fixed: System R fixed everything at compile time; Spanner fixes what to run on each piece of a table at compile time and decides which pieces, and on which servers, at run time. That split is the first half of the lecture. A failure: in System R a crash mid-query simply fails it; Spanner's queries at one timestamp resume where they stopped, which is the last part of the lecture. The storage, replication and transactions underneath are the Oct 19 lecture; we recall only what we need, on slide 6. \n Source: §1, §2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
