    {
      "idx": 9,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two states: SQL beside the store, then SQL inside it, with the takeaway and the TiDB caption. Two ways to add SQL to a store like this. Loosely coupled, left: SQL is a separate service on its own servers, talking to storage through its API, which here means key-range reads, and the rows come up to the SQL tier. F1, the AdWords database from 2012, did exactly that over Spanner. Tightly coupled, right: the compiler and executor live inside the Spanner servers, so the SQL layer knows where the shards are, how they are replicated, and how they move; a server that receives a query ships subplans to the servers holding the data. That is this paper. TiDB, Aug 31, sits in between: its SQL layer runs in its own processes, but it ships single-table plan pieces, filters, aggregates and top-N, into TiKV, its key-value storage layer, and the rest runs in the SQL layer; that pushdown is in the TiDB paper, not in our notes on it. Then the point of the paper in one sentence: making it tight changed both sides. How Spanner stores data changed how queries run, which is distribution, range extraction and restarts, today's core; and what queries need changed how Spanner stores data, which is Ressi, its new storage format, in the lecture notes. Do not oversell tight coupling: the paper's own verdict, in its conclusions, is that both designs work. \n Source: §1; §9. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 10,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two states: SQL beside the store, then SQL inside it, with the takeaway and the TiDB caption. Two ways to add SQL to a store like this. Loosely coupled, left: SQL is a separate service on its own servers, talking to storage through its API, which here means key-range reads, and the rows come up to the SQL tier. F1, the AdWords database from 2012, did exactly that over Spanner. Tightly coupled, right: the compiler and executor live inside the Spanner servers, so the SQL layer knows where the shards are, how they are replicated, and how they move; a server that receives a query ships subplans to the servers holding the data. That is this paper. TiDB, Aug 31, sits in between: its SQL layer runs in its own processes, but it ships single-table plan pieces, filters, aggregates and top-N, into TiKV, its key-value storage layer, and the rest runs in the SQL layer; that pushdown is in the TiDB paper, not in our notes on it. Then the point of the paper in one sentence: making it tight changed both sides. How Spanner stores data changed how queries run, which is distribution, range extraction and restarts, today's core; and what queries need changed how Spanner stores data, which is Ressi, its new storage format, in the lecture notes. Do not oversell tight coupling: the paper's own verdict, in its conclusions, is that both designs work. \n Source: §1; §9. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 11,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. What Spanner already does, from Oct 19, and two things new today: shards move, and a child table can be stored inside its parent."
    },
    {
      "idx": 12,
      "label": "6",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. The shard map every example today uses; tell the class to photograph it. From Oct 19: rows are sorted by primary key and cut into contiguous key ranges, shards, which are TiDB's Regions, Aug 31, under another name. Each shard belongs to one Paxos group, and the group's replicas sit in different datacenters. In Spanner a group holds many shards: here G1 holds S1 and S3, which backup slide 46 uses. In TiDB a Region is its own Raft group, and a Mako shard, Oct 21, its own Paxos group. So keep the two words apart: a shard is a unit of key range, a Paxos group is a unit of replication. Two more things from Oct 19 that come back later, said aloud because the slide does not print them. Read-write transactions use two-phase locking and two-phase commit, and a wound-wait abort, Oct 19's deadlock-avoidance rule, in which an older transaction aborts a younger one holding a lock it wants, already makes the client retry. Reads at a timestamp take no locks: a strong read at the current time, TT.now().latest, the top of TrueTime's uncertainty interval, or a stale read at an older timestamp the client picks, served by any replica whose t_safe, the time up to which it is known to be up to date, has passed that timestamp; the paper calls both snapshot reads. Restarts, at the end of the hour, lean on exactly this. Then the first thing new today, said aloud because no slide shows it: Spanner splits, merges and moves shards between Paxos groups to balance load, at any time, even while a query is reading them, and nothing tells the query; calls are addressed to key ranges, and the coprocessor framework finds whoever owns the range now. Strawman 2, slide 11, fails because of it. Our map, not the paper's: four shards at 250, 500 and 750, three groups. \n Source: §2; Oct 19 notes. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 13,
      "label": "7",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. The second new thing, interleaving, already in the 2012 paper as INTERLEAVE IN PARENT. A child table can be stored inside its parent. Sales' primary key starts with its parent's key, ckey, so in key order each customer is followed by its own sales, as the figure shows. Shard boundaries fall between parent keys, so a customer and all of its sales sit in one shard: the paper says boundaries preserve row co-location, and the rules for which operators can run shard by shard, slides 15 to 19, rely on it. The key prefix that boundaries are cut on is the sharding key, here ckey; it can be several columns, and an interleaved child uses its parent's. Then the link to last time: this is a physical design choice exactly like last lecture's EMP table stored in department-number (DNO) order. The schema author decides what sits together, and some plans become cheap because of it; on slide 19 the Customer–Sales join becomes free of network calls for exactly this reason. The boundary at 249 and 250 is our map's S1 and S2. \n Source: §2; §3.1: interleaving. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 14,
      "label": "8",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. The paper's running query and two obvious ways to run it on many machines, each of which fails for a reason worth remembering."
    },
    {
      "idx": 15,
      "label": "9",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two states: the query, then what it does in words. The paper's Figure 1 query; we call it Q1, and the whole first half runs on it. Read it as English: the five customers, out of a list passed in, with the most global sales. Three details. The array, customer_key_arr, written @arr from now on, is a query parameter: the plan is compiled once, cached, and run many times with different arrays, so the compiler never sees the array; hold on to that, because it kills strawman 2. UNNEST turns the array into rows, so c.ckey IN UNNEST(@arr) means ckey is one of the array's elements. ANY_VALUE(c.name): all rows with one ckey are one customer and share a name, so take any of them. The join is on the whole sharding key, which matters on slide 19. Customer is sharded on ckey and Sales is interleaved in it, so Sales shares the sharding key. \n Source: §3.1, Figure 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 16,
      "label": "9",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two states: the query, then what it does in words. The paper's Figure 1 query; we call it Q1, and the whole first half runs on it. Read it as English: the five customers, out of a list passed in, with the most global sales. Three details. The array, customer_key_arr, written @arr from now on, is a query parameter: the plan is compiled once, cached, and run many times with different arrays, so the compiler never sees the array; hold on to that, because it kills strawman 2. UNNEST turns the array into rows, so c.ckey IN UNNEST(@arr) means ckey is one of the array's elements. ANY_VALUE(c.name): all rows with one ckey are one customer and share a name, so take any of them. The join is on the whole sharding key, which matters on slide 19. Customer is sharded on ckey and Sales is interleaved in it, so Sales shares the sharding key. \n Source: §3.1, Figure 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 17,
      "label": "10",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two states: with the array, then without it, with the takeaway. Strawman 1 is the loosely coupled design in its simplest form: run all of Q1 on one SQL server, its filters, join, aggregation and Top, and read each table through key-range reads against Spanner. With the array it is tolerable: point reads for its few customers and their sales, three shards touched, S3 not at all. Now drop the array and ask for the top five customers overall. The SQL server must pull every Customer row and every Sales row across the network, global or not, because a key-range read cannot test type: type is not a key column, so storage has no way to filter on it. Then it keeps five rows. The filters, the join and the aggregation could all have run next to the data, on each shard, in parallel. That is the takeaway, and it raises the question for the rest of the part: which operators can be shipped, and to where? \n Source: §1; discussion based on §3. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 18,
      "label": "10",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two states: with the array, then without it, with the takeaway. Strawman 1 is the loosely coupled design in its simplest form: run all of Q1 on one SQL server, its filters, join, aggregation and Top, and read each table through key-range reads against Spanner. With the array it is tolerable: point reads for its few customers and their sales, three shards touched, S3 not at all. Now drop the array and ask for the top five customers overall. The SQL server must pull every Customer row and every Sales row across the network, global or not, because a key-range read cannot test type: type is not a key column, so storage has no way to filter on it. Then it keeps five rows. The filters, the join and the aggregation could all have run next to the data, on each shard, in parallel. That is the takeaway, and it raises the question for the rest of the part: which operators can be shipped, and to where? \n Source: §1; discussion based on §3. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 19,
      "label": "11",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Four states: the parallel-database plan, then one reason it fails per click, the takeaway with the last. Strawman 2 is what a classic parallel database does. Partitioning is fixed, so the optimizer can write the plan against it: run this subplan on servers A, B and C, combine at the root. In Spanner the map does not hold still, for three reasons, one per row. Plans are cached and reused, and by the next run S2 may have split into 250 to 400 and 400 to 500 with one half moved to another group. A shard can move during the query itself. And which shards Q1 needs depends on the array, which the compiler has not seen. The last reason has a precedent from last time: System R hit the same wall, a host variable in a stored plan has no value when the plan is chosen, so System R fell back to fixed selectivity guesses from its Table 1, a third for a range. Spanner does not guess; it decides at run time. The paper's sentence: we do not rely on static partitioning during query planning. So the plan says what to run on each piece of a table; which pieces, and where they live, is decided at run time, by key range. \n Source: §2; §3.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 20,
      "label": "11",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Four states: the parallel-database plan, then one reason it fails per click, the takeaway with the last. Strawman 2 is what a classic parallel database does. Partitioning is fixed, so the optimizer can write the plan against it: run this subplan on servers A, B and C, combine at the root. In Spanner the map does not hold still, for three reasons, one per row. Plans are cached and reused, and by the next run S2 may have split into 250 to 400 and 400 to 500 with one half moved to another group. A shard can move during the query itself. And which shards Q1 needs depends on the array, which the compiler has not seen. The last reason has a precedent from last time: System R hit the same wall, a host variable in a stored plan has no value when the plan is chosen, so System R fell back to fixed selectivity guesses from its Table 1, a third for a range. Spanner does not guess; it decides at run time. The paper's sentence: we do not rely on static partitioning during query planning. So the plan says what to run on each piece of a table; which pieces, and where they live, is decided at run time, by key range. \n Source: §2; §3.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
