      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Four states: the parallel-database plan, then one reason it fails per click, the takeaway with the last. Strawman 2 is what a classic parallel database does. Partitioning is fixed, so the optimizer can write the plan against it: run this subplan on servers A, B and C, combine at the root. In Spanner the map does not hold still, for three reasons, one per row. Plans are cached and reused, and by the next run S2 may have split into 250 to 400 and 400 to 500 with one half moved to another group. A shard can move during the query itself. And which shards Q1 needs depends on the array, which the compiler has not seen. The last reason has a precedent from last time: System R hit the same wall, a host variable in a stored plan has no value when the plan is chosen, so System R fell back to fixed selectivity guesses from its Table 1, a third for a range. Spanner does not guess; it decides at run time. The paper's sentence: we do not rely on static partitioning during query planning. So the plan says what to run on each piece of a table; which pieces, and where they live, is decided at run time, by key range. \n Source: §2; §3.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 23,
      "label": "12",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Put distribution into the plan as one operator, then ask how far up the tree it may move. This part contains the lecture's hard turn: partitionability, whether an operator gives the same answer run shard by shard and concatenated, and the five-step walk that distributes Q1. Just before the first join, a slide on how Spanner writes joins, as Apply."
    },
    {
      "idx": 24,
      "label": "13",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three states: the rewrite, then the compiled tree with the caption, then the run-time calls. The fix for strawman 2: make distribution an explicit operator in the algebra. At the start of compilation, put a DistributedUnion directly above every table scan, so a global scan of T becomes a union, over T's shards, of local scans. Its meaning, left half of the figure: for each shard of T, run the subtree on a server hosting that shard, and concatenate the results, in key order when the plan needs it. The plan stays one tree, compiled once. At run time, right half, the thick edge under the union becomes a one-to-N cross-machine call: the subtree goes to the N shards it needs, chosen then, by key range, through the coprocessor framework. The subtree can itself contain a union: multi-level distribution, which the secondary-index query later in the hour uses, backup slide 47. The root server is the server the query was sent to; it runs everything above the outermost union, and any Spanner server can be root. One detail for Q1: an interleaved child has no shards of its own, so Scan(Sales) gets DistributedUnion(Customer), the union over its parent's shards. \n Source: §3.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 25,
      "label": "13",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three states: the rewrite, then the compiled tree with the caption, then the run-time calls. The fix for strawman 2: make distribution an explicit operator in the algebra. At the start of compilation, put a DistributedUnion directly above every table scan, so a global scan of T becomes a union, over T's shards, of local scans. Its meaning, left half of the figure: for each shard of T, run the subtree on a server hosting that shard, and concatenate the results, in key order when the plan needs it. The plan stays one tree, compiled once. At run time, right half, the thick edge under the union becomes a one-to-N cross-machine call: the subtree goes to the N shards it needs, chosen then, by key range, through the coprocessor framework. The subtree can itself contain a union: multi-level distribution, which the secondary-index query later in the hour uses, backup slide 47. The root server is the server the query was sent to; it runs everything above the outermost union, and any Spanner server can be root. One detail for Q1: an interleaved child has no shards of its own, so Scan(Sales) gets DistributedUnion(Customer), the union over its parent's shards. \n Source: §3.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 26,
      "label": "13",
      "overlay": 2,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three states: the rewrite, then the compiled tree with the caption, then the run-time calls. The fix for strawman 2: make distribution an explicit operator in the algebra. At the start of compilation, put a DistributedUnion directly above every table scan, so a global scan of T becomes a union, over T's shards, of local scans. Its meaning, left half of the figure: for each shard of T, run the subtree on a server hosting that shard, and concatenate the results, in key order when the plan needs it. The plan stays one tree, compiled once. At run time, right half, the thick edge under the union becomes a one-to-N cross-machine call: the subtree goes to the N shards it needs, chosen then, by key range, through the coprocessor framework. The subtree can itself contain a union: multi-level distribution, which the secondary-index query later in the hour uses, backup slide 47. The root server is the server the query was sent to; it runs everything above the outermost union, and any Spanner server can be root. One detail for Q1: an interleaved child has no shards of its own, so Scan(Sales) gets DistributedUnion(Customer), the union over its parent's shards. \n Source: §3.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 27,
      "label": "14",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four states: slide 13's compiled tree with a pull-up arrow, then one bullet per click. Optimization now has a direction: pull the union up. Everything below it runs next to the data, on every shard in parallel; everything above it runs on the root, over what came back. Then be honest about last lecture's promise. The recap said the cost measure would grow a network term and a shard fan-out term. The paper writes no cost formula at all. It pulls the union up where possible, its words, as far as partitionability, the next slide, allows; and it cuts calls and rows shipped with rules: pull-up, shard pruning and batching, all coming today, and affinity, running a subtree in-process when its shard is local, a backup slide. Whether anything is ever priced, it does not say. The compiler is described as rewrites via transformation rules: push predicates down, pick indexes, remove subqueries. Join order and statistics are not described at all, and the paper says so in section 8: we have barely touched on query optimization. So today's optimizer is System R's rewrite step plus distribution rules, and System R's search is absent from the paper, not shown to be absent from Spanner. \n Source: §3.1; §8. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
