      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four states: slide 13's compiled tree with a pull-up arrow, then one bullet per click. Optimization now has a direction: pull the union up. Everything below it runs next to the data, on every shard in parallel; everything above it runs on the root, over what came back. Then be honest about last lecture's promise. The recap said the cost measure would grow a network term and a shard fan-out term. The paper writes no cost formula at all. It pulls the union up where possible, its words, as far as partitionability, the next slide, allows; and it cuts calls and rows shipped with rules: pull-up, shard pruning and batching, all coming today, and affinity, running a subtree in-process when its shard is local, a backup slide. Whether anything is ever priced, it does not say. The compiler is described as rewrites via transformation rules: push predicates down, pick indexes, remove subqueries. Join order and statistics are not described at all, and the paper says so in section 8: we have barely touched on query optimization. So today's optimizer is System R's rewrite step plus distribution rules, and System R's search is absent from the paper, not shown to be absent from Spanner. \n Source: §3.1; §8. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
