    {
      "idx": 37,
      "label": "16",
      "overlay": 5,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "3 minutes. Six states: the operators with the question, then one row's verdict per click, the takeaway with the last. Let the room vote on each row before its click. Filter and Project: per row, always. GroupBy on ckey: shard boundaries never fall inside one ckey value, so every group lives in one shard and per-shard groups are final; that needs only co-location, so hash sharding on ckey would do as well, which is our reading, since the paper credits range sharding for both this and sorting. GroupBy on type: one type's rows are spread over every shard, so each shard produces a partial group. Sort by ckey, in the key's direction: sort each shard and concatenate in key order, which needs range sharding, since hash sharding scatters neighbouring keys; last lecture's interesting order, a sort order a later operator can use, shows up here, because a key-ordered union delivers ckey order for free, like a clustered index scan. Top(5): in any order it fails, because N shards each keep five and the union returns up to 5N rows. Ordered by the key, Top is Limit after Sort; the Sort passes and only keep the first five stays above. Ordered by anything else, such as total, it needs a real Top over the N per-shard lists. What happens to the ones that fail is the next slide. \n Source: §3.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 38,
      "label": "17",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four states: the rule and the three operators, then one row's local and final parts per click; the caption comes with Top, the takeaway with the last. What to do with an operator that fails: split it, the well-known multi-stage processing. Write Op as OpFinal after OpLocal; run OpLocal on each shard below the union and OpFinal above it, on the machine running the union: the root, for Q1. The equation says the split plan equals the original. Top(5) is the top five of the per-shard top fives: each of the table's top five rows is in its own shard's top five, because nothing in another shard can push it out. SUM is the sum of the per-shard sums, COUNT the sum of the counts, AVG the sum of the sums over the sum of the counts; never average the averages. A GroupBy whose columns lack the sharding key splits the same way: group locally, then merge the partial groups. One review question to plant: COUNT DISTINCT type cannot ship one number per shard, because the same type appears on several shards; its OpLocal must ship the distinct values themselves, which can be large. \n Source: §3.1: multi-stage processing. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 39,
      "label": "17",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four states: the rule and the three operators, then one row's local and final parts per click; the caption comes with Top, the takeaway with the last. What to do with an operator that fails: split it, the well-known multi-stage processing. Write Op as OpFinal after OpLocal; run OpLocal on each shard below the union and OpFinal above it, on the machine running the union: the root, for Q1. The equation says the split plan equals the original. Top(5) is the top five of the per-shard top fives: each of the table's top five rows is in its own shard's top five, because nothing in another shard can push it out. SUM is the sum of the per-shard sums, COUNT the sum of the counts, AVG the sum of the sums over the sum of the counts; never average the averages. A GroupBy whose columns lack the sharding key splits the same way: group locally, then merge the partial groups. One review question to plant: COUNT DISTINCT type cannot ship one number per shard, because the same type appears on several shards; its OpLocal must ship the distinct values themselves, which can be large. \n Source: §3.1: multi-stage processing. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
