    {
      "idx": 41,
      "label": "17",
      "overlay": 3,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four states: the rule and the three operators, then one row's local and final parts per click; the caption comes with Top, the takeaway with the last. What to do with an operator that fails: split it, the well-known multi-stage processing. Write Op as OpFinal after OpLocal; run OpLocal on each shard below the union and OpFinal above it, on the machine running the union: the root, for Q1. The equation says the split plan equals the original. Top(5) is the top five of the per-shard top fives: each of the table's top five rows is in its own shard's top five, because nothing in another shard can push it out. SUM is the sum of the per-shard sums, COUNT the sum of the counts, AVG the sum of the sums over the sum of the counts; never average the averages. A GroupBy whose columns lack the sharding key splits the same way: group locally, then merge the partial groups. One review question to plant: COUNT DISTINCT type cannot ship one number per shard, because the same type appears on several shards; its OpLocal must ship the distinct values themselves, which can be large. \n Source: §3.1: multi-stage processing. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 42,
      "label": "18",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Before the union meets its first join, how Spanner writes joins, because every plan from here on uses it. CrossApply of input and map: for each row of the input, run the map with that row’s columns bound, and concatenate the results. OuterApply is the left-outer version: it emits the input row with NULLs when the map returns nothing. The paper notes they are common in lambda calculus, and some SQL dialects expose them directly. The link to last time: it runs like the nested-loop join, outer and inner, with the same cost formula. The line under the code is last lecture’s point too: inside the map, the outer row’s columns are constants, so s.ckey = c.ckey becomes s.ckey = 7, column op value, the sargable form, so the map seeks into Sales rather than scanning it. What differs is what the inner can be. Last lecture’s join inner was always one base table, reached by one access path, a scan or one index, and a correlated subquery stayed a separate query block, optimized on its own. Here the map is any plan over the outer row’s columns: a filter, a union over shards, an aggregate, another Apply. The compiler uses these correlated joins throughout, both for algebraic rewrites and as physical operators, and the distribution operators coming up are, algebraically, variants of them. Because the map needs nothing from the input but those column values, it can even be shipped, with a batch of input rows, to each shard the batch touches: DistributedApply, slide 26. \n Source: §2: CrossApply and OuterApply. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 43,
      "label": "19",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three states: the interleaved tables, each customer with its sales in one shard; then the plan with a union under each input; then the merged plan, one union over the join, with the takeaway. Joins have two inputs, so the rule needs two unions. Two unions on the same table, pulled up through an inner or a semi join, merge into one when the equi-join names every sharding-key column. A semi join keeps an input row if a match exists: EXISTS, or IN with a subquery. The figure's before and after: a CrossApply of two unions over Customer becomes one union over Customer of the per-shard CrossApply. It holds when every Sales row that matches a Customer row of shard i is itself in shard i, so no match is ever lost by joining shard by shard. Interleaving guarantees exactly that for Customer and Sales: a customer's sales sit in the customer's shard. The paper says these are usually ancestor-descendant joins in the table hierarchy, executed as local joins on table shards. This is where interleaving pays off: a join with no network calls. If Sales were its own table sharded on ckey, not interleaved, the rule would still need co-location, which interleaving guarantees and separate sharding does not; that case is a review question. \n Source: §3.1: interleaved tables. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 44,
      "label": "19",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three states: the interleaved tables, each customer with its sales in one shard; then the plan with a union under each input; then the merged plan, one union over the join, with the takeaway. Joins have two inputs, so the rule needs two unions. Two unions on the same table, pulled up through an inner or a semi join, merge into one when the equi-join names every sharding-key column. A semi join keeps an input row if a match exists: EXISTS, or IN with a subquery. The figure's before and after: a CrossApply of two unions over Customer becomes one union over Customer of the per-shard CrossApply. It holds when every Sales row that matches a Customer row of shard i is itself in shard i, so no match is ever lost by joining shard by shard. Interleaving guarantees exactly that for Customer and Sales: a customer's sales sit in the customer's shard. The paper says these are usually ancestor-descendant joins in the table hierarchy, executed as local joins on table shards. This is where interleaving pays off: a join with no network calls. If Sales were its own table sharded on ckey, not interleaved, the rule would still need co-location, which interleaving guarantees and separate sharding does not; that case is a review question. \n Source: §3.1: interleaved tables. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 45,
      "label": "19",
      "overlay": 2,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three states: the interleaved tables, each customer with its sales in one shard; then the plan with a union under each input; then the merged plan, one union over the join, with the takeaway. Joins have two inputs, so the rule needs two unions. Two unions on the same table, pulled up through an inner or a semi join, merge into one when the equi-join names every sharding-key column. A semi join keeps an input row if a match exists: EXISTS, or IN with a subquery. The figure's before and after: a CrossApply of two unions over Customer becomes one union over Customer of the per-shard CrossApply. It holds when every Sales row that matches a Customer row of shard i is itself in shard i, so no match is ever lost by joining shard by shard. Interleaving guarantees exactly that for Customer and Sales: a customer's sales sit in the customer's shard. The paper says these are usually ancestor-descendant joins in the table hierarchy, executed as local joins on table shards. This is where interleaving pays off: a join with no network calls. If Sales were its own table sharded on ckey, not interleaved, the rule would still need co-location, which interleaving guarantees and separate sharding does not; that case is a review question. \n Source: §3.1: interleaved tables. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 46,
      "label": "20",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "4 minutes. Six states: the start, then one click per step, each step red in the panel while the union moves. This is the paper's Figure 1, whose dotted lines are the path of the union; here the union itself moves, bottom to top. Start: a union over each scan; Sales has no shards of its own, so both unions are over Customer. Step 1: through the filter on Customer, which is partitionable; because the filter is on the sharding key, the union also keeps a copy of it, its sharding-key filter, for picking shards at run time. Step 2: through the filter on Sales, also partitionable; the union notes s.ckey = c.ckey, meaning the shard it needs is the one holding the input row's ckey. Step 3: at the CrossApply, both unions are over Customer and s.ckey = c.ckey names the whole sharding key, so customer c in shard i has its sales only in shard i: merge, by the rule of slide 19. Step 4: through GroupBy(c.ckey), which groups on the full sharding key: partitionable. Step 5: at Top(5) ordered by total descending, not partitionable; split it, a local Top(5) below the union and the final Top(5) above, and stop. Point out that the per-shard totals are already final, because of step 4, so the local Top ranks true totals, not partial sums. \n Source: §3.1, Figure 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 47,
      "label": "20",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "4 minutes. Six states: the start, then one click per step, each step red in the panel while the union moves. This is the paper's Figure 1, whose dotted lines are the path of the union; here the union itself moves, bottom to top. Start: a union over each scan; Sales has no shards of its own, so both unions are over Customer. Step 1: through the filter on Customer, which is partitionable; because the filter is on the sharding key, the union also keeps a copy of it, its sharding-key filter, for picking shards at run time. Step 2: through the filter on Sales, also partitionable; the union notes s.ckey = c.ckey, meaning the shard it needs is the one holding the input row's ckey. Step 3: at the CrossApply, both unions are over Customer and s.ckey = c.ckey names the whole sharding key, so customer c in shard i has its sales only in shard i: merge, by the rule of slide 19. Step 4: through GroupBy(c.ckey), which groups on the full sharding key: partitionable. Step 5: at Top(5) ordered by total descending, not partitionable; split it, a local Top(5) below the union and the final Top(5) above, and stop. Point out that the per-shard totals are already final, because of step 4, so the local Top ranks true totals, not partial sums. \n Source: §3.1, Figure 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 48,
      "label": "20",
      "overlay": 2,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "4 minutes. Six states: the start, then one click per step, each step red in the panel while the union moves. This is the paper's Figure 1, whose dotted lines are the path of the union; here the union itself moves, bottom to top. Start: a union over each scan; Sales has no shards of its own, so both unions are over Customer. Step 1: through the filter on Customer, which is partitionable; because the filter is on the sharding key, the union also keeps a copy of it, its sharding-key filter, for picking shards at run time. Step 2: through the filter on Sales, also partitionable; the union notes s.ckey = c.ckey, meaning the shard it needs is the one holding the input row's ckey. Step 3: at the CrossApply, both unions are over Customer and s.ckey = c.ckey names the whole sharding key, so customer c in shard i has its sales only in shard i: merge, by the rule of slide 19. Step 4: through GroupBy(c.ckey), which groups on the full sharding key: partitionable. Step 5: at Top(5) ordered by total descending, not partitionable; split it, a local Top(5) below the union and the final Top(5) above, and stop. Point out that the per-shard totals are already final, because of step 4, so the local Top ranks true totals, not partial sums. \n Source: §3.1, Figure 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
