      "note": "4 minutes. Six states: the start, then one click per step, each step red in the panel while the union moves. This is the paper's Figure 1, whose dotted lines are the path of the union; here the union itself moves, bottom to top. Start: a union over each scan; Sales has no shards of its own, so both unions are over Customer. Step 1: through the filter on Customer, which is partitionable; because the filter is on the sharding key, the union also keeps a copy of it, its sharding-key filter, for picking shards at run time. Step 2: through the filter on Sales, also partitionable; the union notes s.ckey = c.ckey, meaning the shard it needs is the one holding the input row's ckey. Step 3: at the CrossApply, both unions are over Customer and s.ckey = c.ckey names the whole sharding key, so customer c in shard i has its sales only in shard i: merge, by the rule of slide 19. Step 4: through GroupBy(c.ckey), which groups on the full sharding key: partitionable. Step 5: at Top(5) ordered by total descending, not partitionable; split it, a local Top(5) below the union and the final Top(5) above, and stop. Point out that the per-shard totals are already final, because of step 4, so the local Top ranks true totals, not partial sums. \n Source: §3.1, Figure 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 51,
      "label": "20",
      "overlay": 5,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "4 minutes. Six states: the start, then one click per step, each step red in the panel while the union moves. This is the paper's Figure 1, whose dotted lines are the path of the union; here the union itself moves, bottom to top. Start: a union over each scan; Sales has no shards of its own, so both unions are over Customer. Step 1: through the filter on Customer, which is partitionable; because the filter is on the sharding key, the union also keeps a copy of it, its sharding-key filter, for picking shards at run time. Step 2: through the filter on Sales, also partitionable; the union notes s.ckey = c.ckey, meaning the shard it needs is the one holding the input row's ckey. Step 3: at the CrossApply, both unions are over Customer and s.ckey = c.ckey names the whole sharding key, so customer c in shard i has its sales only in shard i: merge, by the rule of slide 19. Step 4: through GroupBy(c.ckey), which groups on the full sharding key: partitionable. Step 5: at Top(5) ordered by total descending, not partitionable; split it, a local Top(5) below the union and the final Top(5) above, and stop. Point out that the per-shard totals are already final, because of step 4, so the local Top ranks true totals, not partial sums. \n Source: §3.1, Figure 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 52,
      "label": "21",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The final plan, as the paper draws it. Everything below the union runs on the shards, all of them in parallel, and at most five rows per shard cross the network. The root server runs the union, which makes the calls, and the final Top(5) over those short lists. The sharding-key filter, ckey IN UNNEST(@arr), rides on the union and is what the next slide uses to choose shards. Then the sentence to leave on the board: what made this cheap is the interleaving the schema chose. Without it, Sales has shards of its own, step 3 fails, and the CrossApply becomes a distributed join, which is the next part. \n Source: §3.1, Figure 1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 53,
      "label": "22",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three states: the array, then its points, then the shards they fall in, with the takeaway and caption. The union holds its sharding-key filter, ckey IN UNNEST(@arr). Before calling anyone, it evaluates that filter with this run's parameters into key ranges, using range extraction, from slide 29. Here the array is 7, 412, 7, 950: remove duplicates, the paper does, and you have three points, 7, 412 and 950. Intersect them with the shard map: S1, S2 and S4. S3 is never contacted. That is shard pruning, and it is done per run, which is exactly what strawman 2 could not do at compile time. Why it is safe, which is the caption: the sharding-key filter is implied by the filters of the scans below the union, so every row the subtree could return satisfies it, and therefore lies in one of the extracted ranges. An extra shard would only cost a call that returns nothing; a missing one would lose rows. Range extraction is built on that asymmetry: its ranges may be too big, never too small; why is in the lecture notes, for anyone who asks. \n Source: §3.2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 54,
      "label": "22",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Three states: the array, then its points, then the shards they fall in, with the takeaway and caption. The union holds its sharding-key filter, ckey IN UNNEST(@arr). Before calling anyone, it evaluates that filter with this run's parameters into key ranges, using range extraction, from slide 29. Here the array is 7, 412, 7, 950: remove duplicates, the paper does, and you have three points, 7, 412 and 950. Intersect them with the shard map: S1, S2 and S4. S3 is never contacted. That is shard pruning, and it is done per run, which is exactly what strawman 2 could not do at compile time. Why it is safe, which is the caption: the sharding-key filter is implied by the filters of the scans below the union, so every row the subtree could return satisfies it, and therefore lies in one of the extracted ranges. An extra shard would only cost a call that returns nothing; a missing one would lose rows. Range extraction is built on that asymmetry: its ranges may be too big, never too small; why is in the lecture notes, for anyone who asks. \n Source: §3.2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 55,
      "label": "22",
