      "note": "2 minutes. Three states: the array, then its points, then the shards they fall in, with the takeaway and caption. The union holds its sharding-key filter, ckey IN UNNEST(@arr). Before calling anyone, it evaluates that filter with this run's parameters into key ranges, using range extraction, from slide 29. Here the array is 7, 412, 7, 950: remove duplicates, the paper does, and you have three points, 7, 412 and 950. Intersect them with the shard map: S1, S2 and S4. S3 is never contacted. That is shard pruning, and it is done per run, which is exactly what strawman 2 could not do at compile time. Why it is safe, which is the caption: the sharding-key filter is implied by the filters of the scans below the union, so every row the subtree could return satisfies it, and therefore lies in one of the extracted ranges. An extra shard would only cost a call that returns nothing; a missing one would lose rows. Range extraction is built on that asymmetry: its ranges may be too big, never too small; why is in the lecture notes, for anyone who asks. \n Source: §3.2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 56,
      "label": "23",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Interleaving makes parent-child joins local. A secondary index stored as its own table, sharded on its own key, is the join it cannot cover."
    },
    {
      "idx": 57,
      "label": "24",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Three states: Q2 with the index and Customer's shards, then where each Denver entry's customer lives, then Q2 written as a join. What interleaving does not cover: a global secondary index, which is its own table, sharded on the indexed column. CustomerByCity is stored as a table with key (city, ckey), sharded on city, so one index shard, I2 in the figure, holds all of Denver. Q2 asks for Denver's customers. On one machine Q2 is not even a join: it is last lecture's access path through a non-clustered index, about a page fetch per row found. Here the index is a table of its own, so the access path becomes a join between the index and the base table, with a union over each scan. The SQL at the bottom is Q2 written that way: read CustomerByCity like any table, keep Denver's entries, and join each one to its customer on ckey. Nobody has to write it; the compiler builds this join from Q2 itself, but seeing it as SQL makes the next slides' join plans unsurprising. And the rows the index points at are wherever their ckeys put them: in the figure, five Denver customers live in three of Customer's four shards. Our index shard boundaries, Chicago and Miami, are illustrative. \n Source: §3.3. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 58,
      "label": "24",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Three states: Q2 with the index and Customer's shards, then where each Denver entry's customer lives, then Q2 written as a join. What interleaving does not cover: a global secondary index, which is its own table, sharded on the indexed column. CustomerByCity is stored as a table with key (city, ckey), sharded on city, so one index shard, I2 in the figure, holds all of Denver. Q2 asks for Denver's customers. On one machine Q2 is not even a join: it is last lecture's access path through a non-clustered index, about a page fetch per row found. Here the index is a table of its own, so the access path becomes a join between the index and the base table, with a union over each scan. The SQL at the bottom is Q2 written that way: read CustomerByCity like any table, keep Denver's entries, and join each one to its customer on ckey. Nobody has to write it; the compiler builds this join from Q2 itself, but seeing it as SQL makes the next slides' join plans unsurprising. And the rows the index points at are wherever their ckeys put them: in the figure, five Denver customers live in three of Customer's four shards. Our index shard boundaries, Chicago and Miami, are illustrative. \n Source: §3.3. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 59,
      "label": "24",
      "overlay": 2,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Three states: Q2 with the index and Customer's shards, then where each Denver entry's customer lives, then Q2 written as a join. What interleaving does not cover: a global secondary index, which is its own table, sharded on the indexed column. CustomerByCity is stored as a table with key (city, ckey), sharded on city, so one index shard, I2 in the figure, holds all of Denver. Q2 asks for Denver's customers. On one machine Q2 is not even a join: it is last lecture's access path through a non-clustered index, about a page fetch per row found. Here the index is a table of its own, so the access path becomes a join between the index and the base table, with a union over each scan. The SQL at the bottom is Q2 written that way: read CustomerByCity like any table, keep Denver's entries, and join each one to its customer on ckey. Nobody has to write it; the compiler builds this join from Q2 itself, but seeing it as SQL makes the next slides' join plans unsurprising. And the rows the index points at are wherever their ckeys put them: in the figure, five Denver customers live in three of Customer's four shards. Our index shard boundaries, Chicago and Miami, are illustrative. \n Source: §3.3. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 60,
      "label": "25",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The join as written: a CrossApply whose input is the index scan, city = 'Denver', and whose map looks up the customer with the input row's ckey. The map's union cannot be pulled up: the two tables are sharded differently, so no partitionability rule applies, and it fires once per input row. That is last lecture's nested-loop cost, C equals C outer plus outer rows times C inner, except that C inner is now a call to another machine, not a page fetch. With 1,000 Denver customers that is 1,000 calls, one after another, which the paper calls prohibitively expensive in latency and resources. The other plan, scan all of Customer and filter on city, reads the whole table, which is what the index was for. The figure shows five of the rows, five calls. \n Source: §3.3. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 61,
      "label": "26",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. The fix: send the input rows in batches. This is the paper's Figure 2. Left, the row-at-a-time plan: an Apply, cross or outer, whose map sits under a DistributedUnion, one remote call per input row. Right: one join becomes two. DistributedApply is the apply and the union fused into one operator. On the caller, Batch cuts the input rows into arrays, each named $batch in turn. The subtree rooted at CrossApply is shipped to each shard the batch touches; there, Unnest turns $batch back into rows and the original map runs per row, locally, seeking to its keys rather than scanning. So the distributed join ships batches, and a local join applies each row of a batch on the shard. The paper extends this from equi-joins to inner, left outer and semi joins whose predicates constrain the remote table's sharding keys. \n Source: §3.3, Figure 2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 62,
      "label": "27",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. How a batch is routed, the paper's four steps, done before any call. One: evaluate the map's sharding-key filter, ckey equals the row's ckey, on each row of the batch, which gives each row its key ranges. Two: merge them into one set. Three: intersect with the shard boundaries, which gives the shards to call. Four: per shard, keep only the batch rows whose ranges overlap it; a row whose range spans two shards goes to both. The trace: a batch of five ckeys from the index, 7, 300, 310, 820 and 990. S1 gets 7, S2 gets 300 and 310, S3 is not called, S4 gets 820 and 990: three calls, in parallel, instead of five in a row. Calls per batch is the number of shards touched, not rows, and the paper notes that often the whole input fits in one batch: one round of calls. Then the link to last lecture's Table 2: a non-clustered index paid per row, a clustered one per page; batching makes the index pay per shard touched. On each shard, the per-row map seeks to its keys instead of scanning. \n Source: §3.3: the four steps. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 63,
      "label": "28",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Every step so far needed to know which keys a predicate can touch. This part builds that analysis: the strawman of one key range, then one scan per key column. How Scan2 skips, one predicate's three uses, predicates with OR and NOT, the filter tree and why extraction is safe are in the lecture notes, not on slides."
    },
    {
      "idx": 64,
      "label": "29",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Every step so far needed it: the union's shard list, the per-row ranges of a DistributedApply, and the seek in place of a scan on each shard. Locking needs it too: which keys a read-write transaction must lock. And it is done at run time, because parameters, and the values earlier scans read, are known only then. The paper's example is a Documents table with key ProjectId, DocumentPath, Version, sharded on ProjectId, and a query for one project's documents under /proposals at one version. The paper's values: the parameters are P001 and 2017-01-01, and ten documents sit under /proposals. Ours: 1,000 versions each. For students who read the paper: its text calls the third key column Timestamp once, and Figure 3 calls the second key column DocumentId; both are slips, and the deck uses Version and DocumentPath throughout. \n Source: §4.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 65,
      "label": "30",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two states: the one range, then what we want instead. The strawman is last lecture's index scan: one key range, from a start key to a stop key. By last lecture's definition all three factors, the ANDed conditions, match: they are sargable, and their columns form a prefix of the key. But only equalities on the leading columns, then one range, narrow the scan, and that fine print is new today. Here ProjectId = P001 is an equality, then DocumentPath in the range from /proposals to /proposalt, the next string after the prefix, is a range. Version = @param2 comes after the range, so it is tested on every version read and narrows nothing. With 1,000 versions per document and ten documents, that is 10,000 rows read to return 10: a thousand times the rows needed. What we want instead: for each distinct DocumentPath in the range, one seek to its 2017-01-01 version. \n Source: §4.1; Selinger §4: matching index. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 66,
      "label": "30",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Two states: the one range, then what we want instead. The strawman is last lecture's index scan: one key range, from a start key to a stop key. By last lecture's definition all three factors, the ANDed conditions, match: they are sargable, and their columns form a prefix of the key. But only equalities on the leading columns, then one range, narrow the scan, and that fine print is new today. Here ProjectId = P001 is an equality, then DocumentPath in the range from /proposals to /proposalt, the next string after the prefix, is a range. Version = @param2 comes after the range, so it is tested on every version read and narrows nothing. With 1,000 versions per document and ten documents, that is 10,000 rows read to return 10: a thousand times the rows needed. What we want instead: for each distinct DocumentPath in the range, one seek to its 2017-01-01 version. \n Source: §4.1; Selinger §4: matching index. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 67,
      "label": "31",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Six states, building the tree bottom up, each new box red: Scan1; Scan2, which needs a project it does not have; the lower CrossApply, which runs Scan2 once per project Scan1 emits; Scan3, which needs a path; the upper CrossApply, which runs Scan3 once per path; then the whole tree with a summary. Pin one key column, then constrain the next given its value: a nested loop over key columns. The compiler rewrites the filtered scan into a chain of CrossApplys over the same table, one scan per key column, the paper's Figure 3. Read the boxes as each scan's own attributes: its filter and what it emits, not children. Scan1 handles ProjectId; the filter pins it to @param1, so it reads nothing and emits @project_id. Scan2, correlated on @project_id, handles DocumentPath under the prefix and emits one @document_path per distinct path. Scan3, correlated on both, seeks the full key, path and version, and emits the rows. It is Apply again, slide 18, with the outer row's values as constants inside the map, which is what lets each inner scan seek. \n Source: §4.2, Figure 3. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 68,
      "label": "31",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Six states, building the tree bottom up, each new box red: Scan1; Scan2, which needs a project it does not have; the lower CrossApply, which runs Scan2 once per project Scan1 emits; Scan3, which needs a path; the upper CrossApply, which runs Scan3 once per path; then the whole tree with a summary. Pin one key column, then constrain the next given its value: a nested loop over key columns. The compiler rewrites the filtered scan into a chain of CrossApplys over the same table, one scan per key column, the paper's Figure 3. Read the boxes as each scan's own attributes: its filter and what it emits, not children. Scan1 handles ProjectId; the filter pins it to @param1, so it reads nothing and emits @project_id. Scan2, correlated on @project_id, handles DocumentPath under the prefix and emits one @document_path per distinct path. Scan3, correlated on both, seeks the full key, path and version, and emits the rows. It is Apply again, slide 18, with the outer row's values as constants inside the map, which is what lets each inner scan seek. \n Source: §4.2, Figure 3. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
