      "overlay": 5,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Six states, building the tree bottom up, each new box red: Scan1; Scan2, which needs a project it does not have; the lower CrossApply, which runs Scan2 once per project Scan1 emits; Scan3, which needs a path; the upper CrossApply, which runs Scan3 once per path; then the whole tree with a summary. Pin one key column, then constrain the next given its value: a nested loop over key columns. The compiler rewrites the filtered scan into a chain of CrossApplys over the same table, one scan per key column, the paper's Figure 3. Read the boxes as each scan's own attributes: its filter and what it emits, not children. Scan1 handles ProjectId; the filter pins it to @param1, so it reads nothing and emits @project_id. Scan2, correlated on @project_id, handles DocumentPath under the prefix and emits one @document_path per distinct path. Scan3, correlated on both, seeks the full key, path and version, and emits the rows. It is Apply again, slide 18, with the outer row's values as constants inside the map, which is what lets each inner scan seek. \n Source: §4.2, Figure 3. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 73,
      "label": "32",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. What happens when something breaks in the middle of a long query, three strawmen, and the restart token that replaced them."
    },
    {
      "idx": 74,
      "label": "33",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four states: the query and its stream, then one failure per click, the takeaway with the last. The scenario: a long query reads 50 shards at one snapshot timestamp and streams a million rows to the client over half a minute; the client has consumed 400,000. Then one of three things happens, and at Spanner's scale one of them always does. A server it reads from restarts: Spanner is always upgrading somewhere, and the paper reports very few moments when no zone, one deployment of Spanner servers, was being upgraded. A shard it reads splits, and half moves to another Paxos group: data movement. Or a later or retried call lands on a replica that is overloaded, or not yet caught up to the query's timestamp, t_safe below the read timestamp; rather than block, the call gives up and retries at another replica, which the paper calls distributed wait. The question for the next slide: what should the system do with 400,000 rows already delivered? \n Source: §5.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 75,
      "label": "33",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four states: the query and its stream, then one failure per click, the takeaway with the last. The scenario: a long query reads 50 shards at one snapshot timestamp and streams a million rows to the client over half a minute; the client has consumed 400,000. Then one of three things happens, and at Spanner's scale one of them always does. A server it reads from restarts: Spanner is always upgrading somewhere, and the paper reports very few moments when no zone, one deployment of Spanner servers, was being upgraded. A shard it reads splits, and half moves to another Paxos group: data movement. Or a later or retried call lands on a replica that is overloaded, or not yet caught up to the query's timestamp, t_safe below the read timestamp; rather than block, the call gives up and retries at another replica, which the paper calls distributed wait. The question for the next slide: what should the system do with 400,000 rows already delivered? \n Source: §5.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
