    {
      "idx": 81,
      "label": "34",
      "overlay": 3,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Four states: the three strawmen, then each one's cost, one per click. Return an error, and let the client retry from the start: every application writes a retry loop with backoff, and those loops are a known source of bugs; a snapshot transaction whose reads are spread across modules of an application cannot be retried cleanly; a query that runs about as long as the time between failures may never finish before its snapshot is garbage-collected, retry loop or not; and one bad shard sets the latency of a query that touched fifty. Make results durable: buffer the results, or each operator's intermediate output, in storage as reliable as the database, and resume from there; every query pays latency to protect the few that fail. Rewrite the query so it can resume: tag every intermediate row with an identity column, or page by key, WHERE col > @last ORDER BY col LIMIT n. The paper says identity columns looked impossible in the general case; our guess at why is that aggregates and joins make rows with no identity of their own. Paging adds a sort the query did not otherwise need. The insight: the query processor itself knows how far it got. Have it say so, cheaply, with every batch of results. \n Source: §5.1. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 82,
      "label": "35",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Every batch of results carries an opaque restart token. To resume, the client library resends the original request plus the last token it received; the application never sees it. The calls between Spanner servers, a union's calls to its shards, are restartable the same way. The contract is weaker than you might expect, and that is deliberate: the rows before and after a restart together are a correct answer, none returned twice, but there is no promise that it is the answer an uninterrupted run would have given. The caption's example: SELECT * FROM T LIMIT 100, restarted after 30 rows, returns 70 more rows, none repeated, not necessarily the 70 the first attempt would have sent. Restarts stream: nothing is written to persistent storage for their sake. The simplest case, a range scan with filters and projections: the token is the last primary key returned plus the snapshot timestamp, and resuming is a seek just past that key, at that timestamp. Why the timestamp is the next slide. \n Source: §5.2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 83,
      "label": "36",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two states: a row returned twice, then a row never returned, with the takeaway and caption. The part after the restart must read the same state as the part before. Suppose a scan stopped at key 50 at timestamp 100, and resumes, without the timestamp, at a newer one, 120. A transaction at 110 that deleted the row at key 10, already returned, and reinserted its data at key 90, not yet reached, makes it show up twice. One that moved a row from 90 to 10 makes it vanish: at 100 it was at 90, not yet reached, and at 120 it is at 10, already passed. Keep the timestamp in the token and both halves read the same snapshot. Every replica whose t_safe has passed that timestamp returns the same data, Oct 19, so the resume may run on any of them, even in another datacenter. Strong reads are covered too: their timestamp is picked once, at the start, and travels in the token. The limit: old versions are garbage-collected. Snapshot data has been garbage collected is an error the client still sees, as is deadline exceeded. \n Source: §5.2; Oct 19 notes. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 84,
      "label": "36",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two states: a row returned twice, then a row never returned, with the takeaway and caption. The part after the restart must read the same state as the part before. Suppose a scan stopped at key 50 at timestamp 100, and resumes, without the timestamp, at a newer one, 120. A transaction at 110 that deleted the row at key 10, already returned, and reinserted its data at key 90, not yet reached, makes it show up twice. One that moved a row from 90 to 10 makes it vanish: at 100 it was at 90, not yet reached, and at 120 it is at 10, already passed. Keep the timestamp in the token and both halves read the same snapshot. Every replica whose t_safe has passed that timestamp returns the same data, Oct 19, so the resume may run on any of them, even in another datacenter. Strong reads are covered too: their timestamp is picked once, at the start, and travels in the token. The limit: old versions are garbage-collected. Snapshot data has been garbage collected is an error the client still sees, as is deadline exceeded. \n Source: §5.2; Oct 19 notes. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 85,
      "label": "37",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Who gets restarts. Snapshot queries, strong ones included, are most of Spanner's queries, and they all do, because a snapshot timestamp pins what they read. Read-write transactions do not. Their reads have no snapshot timestamp: they read the latest data, which stays valid only while their locks are held. Those locks, taken as the transaction runs, live only in the leader's memory; what goes through Paxos is the prepare record. A failure often loses the locks, and another transaction may then change rows already read, so there is no state to resume into. The transaction aborts and the client retries, as it already must after a wound-wait abort, Oct 19. \n Source: §5.2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 86,
      "label": "38",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four states: server A stops after key 24, then the token it leaves, then the merged shard on server B, then the resume with the takeaway. The paper names what makes tokens hard and defers the mechanisms to another paper; here is what a token has to capture. First, resharding, on the paper's own ranges; the timestamp and keys are ours: server A serves the range 20 to 30 at timestamp 105 and has returned keys 20 through 24 when it restarts. The token records timestamp 105 and that the range 20 to 30 is done up to key 24. Meanwhile 10 to 40 has become one shard, on server B. The resume, still addressed to 20 to 30, reaches B, which seeks just past 24, stops at 30, and reads at 105. A token that said shard 3, 400 rows done would mean nothing on B, which has a different shard with different rows in it: progress must be recorded in key space. \n Source: §5.1: dynamic resharding; §5.2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 87,
      "label": "38",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four states: server A stops after key 24, then the token it leaves, then the merged shard on server B, then the resume with the takeaway. The paper names what makes tokens hard and defers the mechanisms to another paper; here is what a token has to capture. First, resharding, on the paper's own ranges; the timestamp and keys are ours: server A serves the range 20 to 30 at timestamp 105 and has returned keys 20 through 24 when it restarts. The token records timestamp 105 and that the range 20 to 30 is done up to key 24. Meanwhile 10 to 40 has become one shard, on server B. The resume, still addressed to 20 to 30, reaches B, which seeks just past 24, stops at 30, and reads at 105. A token that said shard 3, 400 rows done would mean nothing on B, which has a different shard with different rows in it: progress must be recorded in key space. \n Source: §5.1: dynamic resharding; §5.2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 88,
      "label": "38",
      "overlay": 2,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
