      "note": "1 minute. Four states: server A stops after key 24, then the token it leaves, then the merged shard on server B, then the resume with the takeaway. The paper names what makes tokens hard and defers the mechanisms to another paper; here is what a token has to capture. First, resharding, on the paper's own ranges; the timestamp and keys are ours: server A serves the range 20 to 30 at timestamp 105 and has returned keys 20 through 24 when it restarts. The token records timestamp 105 and that the range 20 to 30 is done up to key 24. Meanwhile 10 to 40 has become one shard, on server B. The resume, still addressed to 20 to 30, reaches B, which seeks just past 24, stops at 30, and reads at 105. A token that said shard 3, 400 rows done would mean nothing on B, which has a different shard with different rows in it: progress must be recorded in key space. \n Source: §5.1: dynamic resharding; §5.2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 90,
      "label": "39",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Four lessons from running Spanner in production, one figure each, before the recap: users wanted SQL, plans must not regress, scale first, then go relational, and SQL versus NoSQL with one size fits all. The lecture notes have the full list."
    },
    {
      "idx": 91,
      "label": "40",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Four lessons from running Spanner in production, before the recap; the lecture notes have the full list. First, users wanted SQL. The key-value API, point lookups and range scans, was the quick way to launch and still serves simple retrieval, but SQL expressed the complex access patterns and pushed computation to the data, which is this whole lecture. Adoption jumped once Google had one dialect: SQL query volume doubled in the year before the common dialect and grew five times in the year after, so the bars are 1, 2 and 10. In that year the internal distribution calls in queries grew fourfold, which the paper reads as queries getting more complex. Prospective Cloud customers were overwhelmingly interested in SQL. How one dialect was built and kept the same across engines is in the lecture notes. \n Source: §8. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 92,
      "label": "41",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Second, the optimizer must not make things worse. Spanner ships a new version roughly every two weeks, and it backs mission-critical systems, so any performance regression is unwelcome; regressions caused by the optimizer are among the most sensitive, with possible swings of an order of magnitude. Tie it to last lecture: a cost-based optimizer picks the cheapest estimated plan, so improve a rule or an estimate and the winner changes, faster for most queries and much slower for a few. The curves are illustrative, not measured. The paper calls innovating in optimization while keeping workloads stable a big challenge and says little more; Cloud Spanner today lets an application pin the optimizer version its queries use. \n Source: §8. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 93,
      "label": "42",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Third, the path and the advice. Spanner reached SQL one milestone at a time: scalability; manageability, which for Spanner meant transparent failover across clusters and easy resharding as the system grows; ACID transactions across rows; the relational model, schema DDL with indexes on nested data, and finally SQL. Scale came first: horizontal scaling got Spanner deployed without heavy investment in single-machine speed, which came later, with Ressi. The advice to anyone on the same path is the red arrow, which leaves after manageability: once a scalable, available storage core exists, adopt the relational model early, because well-known relational abstractions speed up development and make the migrations you will eventually face cheaper. That closes the loop with slide 2: a key-value store first, then a full SQL database. \n Source: §8; §9. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 94,
      "label": "43",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Fourth, two debates the paper takes a side on. Left, SQL versus NoSQL. NoSQL was born of the need for highly available systems at scale and low cost; scalability is never compromised at a web company, and a scalable system must address manageability early, which for Spanner meant transparent failover and easy resharding. Those properties can come from a key-value store with no SQL at all, Bigtable for instance. Cross-row ACID transactions are the next-hardest barrier, and a scalable, manageable, transactional key-value store is, in the paper's words, perhaps the lowest common denominator for building enterprise-strength global storage systems. F1 showed that SQL can be built on such a core, loosely; this paper builds it in, tightly. So the paper's conclusion: from where many Google engineers sit, the SQL versus NoSQL dichotomy may no longer be relevant. It is a stack. Right, one size fits all. Stonebraker and Cetintemel argued in 2005 that one engine for every workload is an idea whose time has come and gone: specialised engines win. The paper's answer from customers: combining OLTP, OLAP and full-text search in one system remains at the top of their priorities, because deploying and monitoring several systems, each with its own availability guarantees, transaction semantics, rollout cycle and API quirks, is a major burden. Spanner's stated goal is to perform well across that range over time, a goal, not a claim that it already does. Counterpoint for discussion: TiDB, Aug 31, answers the OLTP-plus-analytics part of that demand with two copies of the data, rows and columns. \n Source: §8; §9. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 95,
      "label": "44",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The end of the main route, and no new content: one topic per part of the hour, each with a few reminder words; say the line behind each. Distribution: a DistributedUnion over every scan, pulled up while partitionability holds, slides 13 to 16, with Top and aggregates split into local and final parts, slide 17. Locality: interleaving makes the parent-child join local, slide 19, and DistributedApply batches the joins it cannot cover, slides 24 to 27. Run time: shard pruning, slide 22; one call per Paxos group and affinity, running a subtree in-process when its shard is local, are backup slide 46. Range extraction: one scan per key column, slides 29 to 31, and one predicate's ranges for routing, seeks and locks, said on slide 29; how Scan2 skips, the three uses, and why the ranges may be too big but never too small are in the lecture notes, for anyone who asks. Restarts: a token per batch, a snapshot timestamp and progress in key space, slides 35 to 38. Lessons: users wanted SQL, plans must not regress, scale first, then go relational, and SQL versus NoSQL as a stack while customers still want one system, slides 40 to 43. Then the result for Q1: everything below the union runs next to the data, slide 21. The three slides to re-read: 16, which operators pass; 27, why batching makes an index pay per shard; and 31, how one scan per key column turns a predicate into seeks. Forward: Nov 11 is isolation levels weaker than Spanner's serializability, phantoms included; Nov 16 is CockroachDB, range-sharded key-value storage with SQL in the same process. Five backup slides follow, slides 46 to 50, for spare time; the review questions are at the end of the lecture notes. \n Source: §3–§5, §8–§9, assembled. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 96,
      "label": "45",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Backup, 0 minutes. Five slides for spare time, each self-contained: one call per Paxos group and shard affinity; Q2's two-level plan; location hints, which send a query straight to the server holding its data; parallel consumers, several workers splitting one query's results; and locking rows that are not there yet, a preview of Nov 11. Use any of them in any order."
    },
    {
      "idx": 97,
      "label": "46",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Backup, 1 minute. Fewer calls, two ways. Left: when the extracted ranges cover the whole table, for example Q1 with no array, asking for the top five customers overall, the union sends one call per Paxos group rather than one per shard, since a group hosts several shards of the same table. In our map G1 hosts S1 and S3, so four shards cost three calls. Calls go out in parallel, up to a cap that keeps one query from swamping the servers, and a big shard can be split further into sub-shards, using split points the storage layer keeps, to parallelize within it. Right: shard affinity. If a target shard is hosted on the server running the union, the subtree runs in-process, with no call at all. The paper says this is common and important: many queries read one object, a user and its interleaved rows, under one sharding key, and most such row sets fit in one shard. \n Source: §3.2. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 98,
      "label": "47",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Backup, 1 minute. Q2's final plan: two levels of distribution. The index's own union, DistributedUnion(CustomerByCity), is pulled above the DistributedApply, because the apply's map runs once per input row, which behaves like a Filter for partitionability; that rule is our reading, and the lecture notes' table of rules lists it. Its sharding-key filter, city = 'Denver', picks the index shard or shards holding Denver, and the DistributedApply runs on each of them, batching the index entries and calling the Customer shards each batch touches. Where batching fits: the input is small, and the join condition constrains the map table's sharding key, by equality or range: index to base table, foreign key to parent; inner, left outer and semi joins. Where it does not: two big tables joined on a non-key column; there is nothing to route on, so every batch goes to every shard. The paper calls distributed joins too large to cover exhaustively and shows only this one. \n Source: §3.3; §3.4. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 99,
      "label": "48",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Backup, 1 minute. Single consumer: one client reads all the results. The query goes first to a root server, which orchestrates the rest. If that root holds none of the data, it costs an extra hop: a point lookup that needs one cross-machine call makes two. So Spanner routes the query to the data, by location hint. On the first execution, the compiled plan is analyzed for the outermost distribution operator with a sharding-key expression; that expression, written in terms of the query's parameters, is turned into a pattern and cached on the client. On later runs the client plugs in the parameters, gets a key, and addresses the query to that key; the coprocessor framework finds the server. Two examples. A customer and its sales by ckey: the hint is Customer key = @k, so the query goes to the server holding customer @k, and since Sales is interleaved the whole query runs there, in-process. Customers by city: the hint is the index's key, so the query goes to the server holding that index shard. The paper's examples use tables named Parent, Child and Index; the shapes are the same. \n Source: §3.4: single-consumer API. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 100,
      "label": "49",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Backup, 1 minute. Parallel consumer: results too big for one machine, for example a MapReduce-style pipeline that joins Spanner data with data from other systems, but still wants filtering done next to the data. Two stages. First, send the query and the parallelism wanted, here two, and get back opaque partition descriptors. Second, workers each run one descriptor, calling the shards directly; together they return the single-consumer result, up to order, all at one snapshot timestamp. With Customer's four shards and two partitions, each descriptor covers two shards. This works only for root-partitionable queries: the compiled plan's root is a DistributedUnion, so a descriptor is just a subset of its shards. Q1 is not root-partitionable: its final Top(5) sits above the union, and no single worker can compute it. Cloud Spanner exposes this today as PartitionQuery. \n Source: §3.4: parallel-consumer API. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    },
    {
      "idx": 101,
      "label": "50",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Backup, 1 minute. Spanner locks per column, and a key column and a non-key column stand for different things. The key columns of a range stand for which rows exist there, so an insert or delete in the range conflicts. A non-key column of a row stands for its value, so an update of that row conflicts. Why the key-column lock covers the whole range, which is our reading: the answer also depends on which documents are absent. Oct 19's two-phase locking locks the items read, but /proposals/doc11 is not an item yet, so lock the range its insert would land in. A row that appears in a range you already read is a phantom, Nov 11. Snapshot reads lock nothing; for them the ranges say which prepared, uncommitted writes to wait out, rather than every one in the Paxos group, Oct 19's t_safe, a refinement already in the 2012 paper. The paper leaves a trade-off open: fine ranges read and lock less, but cost more to compute and to hold. \n Source: §4.1; Oct 19 notes; Nov 11 preview. https://doi.org/10.1145/3035918.3056103"
    }
  ]
}
